PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: This is a coherent, well-themed 120-level set. Each chapter has one clear mechanic, a boss, a breather and a Masterwork, and the bot win rates follow the studio targets (casual 100% down to about 54%).

Weaknesses:
- Early chapters are repetitive "exact row, then column" reads.
- Reach levels L13-L24 reuse the same insight.
- A few levels are too small for their role: L41 is a thin "twist", and L45 is a hard "teach".
- Some pictures carry empty rows and columns that add no deduction.
- Several late levels stack three mechanics at once.
- Breathers cluster at 90-93% casual, followed by a long run of 55% levels with little relief.
- Few levels reach the "memorable" band. Strong pictures such as L12, L24, L36, L48, L60, L84, L108 and L120 carry the set. Curve: The curve is well structured. Each chapter introduces one mechanic: line logic, reach, colour, void, stamp, colour-cross, smudged, cracked, deep-chain, then pairings in the Grand Exhibit. Each chapter has a boss, a breather and a Masterwork finale, and the wins stay on target (casual falls from about 100% to 54%). Problems:
1. Chapter 1 spends 12 levels on overlap, exact-fit and X-marks with little escalation. L1-L2 and L7 are nearly the same "exact row, then column" read.
2. The reach chapter (L13-L24) repeats one insight ("run must touch the anchor") 12 times. Levels 14, 17, 19 and 21 blur together.
3. The four breathers in Pigment Market, Sculpture Hall, Conservatory and Mosaic Hall (L31, L43, L55, L67) all sit at 90-93% casual. They are fine individually, but each breather is followed by a flat run before the next spike.
4. Colour is introduced on a very small 7x7 grid with clues that nearly give the answer. It should show the touching rule with a clearer visual contrast.
5. The cracked-clue chapter's first level (L85) is well taught. Later cracked levels (L90, L93, L96) stack colour, cracked and chain at once, so they are heavy for a mechanic's third or fourth outing.
6. L41 and L45 are the weakest points: L41 is trivially short for a "twist" and L45 is a teach level at 79% casual.
7. Late chapters (L97-L120) run at 53-60% casual for 24 levels with few breathers. The only breathers are L103, L115 and L79, so the fatigue risk is high even though the targets are met.
8. Many pictures have 2-3 empty or filler rows and columns, which adds clutter rather than deduction.
Overall the set is polished, with strong themed pictures, but it is sound rather than inspired because aha moments repeat.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L41 (6/10): The twist is thin: 2 passes, 16 moves, and the void centre removes most of the board so the level is trivial. → Add a second pocket gap or make the wreath leaves partial so columns 1-3 need chains.
L45 (6/10): Two mechanics in a teach level at 79% casual, which is below the target for a teach; the rest is mechanical. → Teach one at a time: make this colour-only on a smaller void, and move the pairing to L47.
L53 (6/10): 8 passes chain is long and mechanical, and 74% casual; the stamped paper cell is easy to miss visually. → Render stamped paper clearly and cut the chain by adding a mid-board exact row.
L81 (6/10): Casual 64% and many scattered dots; stamp in the smudged column is hard to read as a clue, so it can feel like guessing. → Make the stamp visually distinct and reduce dot noise in the middle rows.
L82 (6/10): Casual 55% with the board being three long full rows; the hidden row is an open guess, and it is lopsided against its neighbours. → Add one column anchor that fixes row 8's start and trim the sky.
L88 (6/10): The picture (a plate with a boat) does not match its title or intent and the board is mostly a symmetric ring. → Redraw the plate so the cracked run is the aha, and make the picture asymmetrical.
L2 (7/10): Exact-fit row 4 hands over the whole solve; little aha beyond L1, and it feels like L1 again. → Make the lone-1 columns less symmetric, or add one cell that needs an X from a finished column.
L3 (7/10): Only 5x6 and the overlap is trivial; no real insight beyond the intent line. → Use a 7x7 where overlap is needed on both axes before any fill becomes obvious.
L5 (7/10): Labelled twist but the tail rows are mostly mechanical once row 5 is boxed. → Remove one symmetric leg so the starfish is asymmetrical and needs one extra chain step.
L7 (7/10): Breather after a 83% boss is okay, but the board is almost identical to L2 and L1 (full row 3 plus the full edge column). → Change the picture's rim or spade so one column needs overlap, not just an exact fit.
L9 (7/10): The anchor is fine but the left lower rows are empty and the arms read as busywork. → Trim the dead bottom row 9 (use 8 rows), or put the flukes in it.
L10 (7/10): Combine level that plays close to L9; it adds nothing the previous one did not. → Make row 4's 9 and row 6's 8 overlap alone before the exact row so the order of attack is a choice.
L14 (7/10): Two ideas (non-mirror and reach) in a 'teach'; fox picture has lopsided rows that clutter. → Keep reach only; use a mirrored fox so the teach is single-idea.
L16 (7/10): Repeats L15 logic with a perch row; top rows are scattered single cells and add clutter. → Fill in spines (solid rows of 3) so top rows carry real clues.
L17 (7/10): Called twist but is another perch-reach read of L15/L16. → Make the twist real: add a void or a gap that breaks the anchor row.
L19 (7/10): Fine breather (93%), but it follows a 79% boss with the same row-exact idea used in L14. → Swap in a different anchor, such as a column-exact line, for variety.
L21 (7/10): Not clearly a combine; it feels like L14 plus L15 with no new interaction. → Add a case where a reach X contradicts an overlap guess.
L23 (7/10): Fern is sparse with many half-empty rows; 9 passes on 5 open lines is a long mechanical chain. → Add a few leaflets to cut passes to 6 and fill the empty left half.
L25 (7/10): Colour teach on a 7x7 whose clues are nearly exact; the touching rule is stated but not tested. → Add one line where a wrong gap assumption fails.
L29 (7/10): Twist is a single exact row; a tiny 8x9 and overall easy for a twist. → Put the bruise in a column that has a competing placement so the aha is real.
L32 (7/10): Several rows have a single lone cell (row 7); the picture wastes space on the left. → Widen the pot or leaf to use the empty cols 1-3.
L42 (7/10): Boss spike at 73% casual relative to neighbours, but the right half of top rows is voids with little ink; board use is thin. → Trim the void area or add a rider on the right so the horse uses more of the canvas.
L47 (7/10): Casual 75% for a combine is hard; the plinth's off-by-one overlap is a guess-risk if players miss the colour edge. → Add a one-cell clue anchor on the plinth edge to remove any guess.
L49 (7/10): Introduces stamp but the stamp is on row 1 where the clue gives it away; the lower half is a bare chain. → Put the stamp where it disambiguates a line that otherwise has two solutions.
L51 (7/10): Second near-identical stamp on a snout; only 14 moves, easy for a practise. → Use two stamps or a stamp in the interior.

